---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import PageNext from '../../components/PageNext.astro';
import CtaBlock from '../../components/CtaBlock.astro';
import CapabilityMatrix from '../../components/CapabilityMatrix.astro';
---

<Layout title="Máddu vs LangGraph" pathname="/compare/langgraph">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="eyebrow-chain">
        <span class="eyebrow">Compare</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">agent frameworks</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">not our market</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Máddu vs LangGraph. <span class="dim-h1">Different layers of the stack.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        If you're building programmable agent graphs, durable state machines, or human-in-the-loop interrupts, LangGraph does that far better than Máddu ever will. The split is simpler than a feature fight: LangGraph is what you build <em>inside</em>; Máddu sits <em>outside</em>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 01 · What LangGraph is</span>
        <h2>A framework for building agents.</h2>
        <p>
          LangGraph is a code-first framework for authoring agent applications as graphs: nodes, edges, shared state, streaming, human-in-the-loop interrupts, and time-travel debugging, all on its own runtime. Its improvement loop reaches into the cloud — LangSmith ingests traces to hosted sandboxes, and Context Hub is a cloud SaaS repository. It has a large, mature ecosystem, and it is genuinely excellent at what it does. Máddu will never compete on that ground; nor should it.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="vs-honest">
        <h4>Where LangGraph is stronger — plainly</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Graph orchestration, shared state, and streaming as first-class building blocks.</li>
          <li>Human-in-the-loop interrupts and time-travel debugging baked into the runtime.</li>
          <li>A huge ecosystem — integrations, docs, and a hosted platform for the improvement loop.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 02 · Where Máddu differs</span>
        <h2>You build agents in a framework. You govern them from outside one.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Outside, not inside</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">zero code</span>
          </div>
          <p>LangGraph is a runtime your agent lives in — you write code against it. Máddu wraps whatever CLI agent you already run (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, Aider) without a single line of application code. It governs the work; it isn't the work.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Local, not cloud</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">no infrastructure</span>
          </div>
          <p>LangGraph's improvement loop is cloud-shaped: traces to LangSmith sandboxes, a SaaS Context Hub. Máddu's learn loop mines local transcripts and writes corrections to files on disk — <code>maddu learn</code> stays on your machine. No cloud, ever.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">A record, not a graph</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">tamper-evident</span>
          </div>
          <p>A framework produces application behavior. Máddu produces a governance record: a hash-chained, append-only spine in <code>.maddu/</code>, verifiable with <code>maddu spine verify</code> — what was allowed, claimed, gated, and verified, owned by the repo.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Callout tone="accent" title="Not a competitor — a different question" tag="the real split">
        <p>
          LangGraph (with CrewAI and the OpenAI Agents SDK) answers "how do I build an agent?" Máddu answers "how do I govern the coding agents already working on my repo?" You can build with LangGraph and still want the second answer — they don't overlap, and Máddu will never pretend they do.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <CapabilityMatrix
        players={['langgraph']}
        eyebrow="§ 03 · The matrix"
        title="Axis by axis."
        subtitle="The fifteen research axes, focused on this matchup. Conservative marks — shipped, documented surfaces only."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 04 · Choose honestly</span>
        <h2>Two defensible choices.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Choose LangGraph when</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="vs-list">
            <li>You're building an agent product — graphs, durable state, interrupts</li>
            <li>You want time-travel debugging and streaming as runtime primitives</li>
            <li>A hosted platform for the trace-and-improve loop is a feature, not a cost</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Choose Máddu when</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="vs-list">
            <li>You're governing CLI coding agents working on your existing repos</li>
            <li>The governance record must live in the repo, with zero code to add</li>
            <li>No cloud is a requirement — the learn loop stays entirely on disk</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="feat-note">
        Related: <a href="/features/autonomy">earned autonomy</a> · <a href="/features/learn">the learn loop</a> · <a href="/hard-rules">the hard rules</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PageNext
    eyebrow="Next comparison"
    title="Máddu + Langfuse."
    hint="Traces tell you what the model did; the spine tells you what was allowed."
    href="/compare/langfuse"
    tone="accent"
  />
  <CtaBlock />
</Layout>

<style>
  .vs-honest {
    padding: var(--m-space-4) var(--m-space-5);
    background: var(--m-bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--m-line);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--m-warn);
    border-radius: var(--m-radius);
    max-width: 720px;
  }
  .vs-honest h4 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--m-space-2);
    font-family: var(--m-font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--m-warn);
  }
  .vs-honest ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; }
  .vs-honest li { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--m-fg-1); }
  .vs-list { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
  .vs-list li { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--m-fg-1); line-height: 1.5; }
  .feat-note {
    margin-top: var(--m-space-5);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--m-fg-2);
  }
  .feat-note code { font-size: 12px; }
</style>
